    # 168 - 209: Flower, Inflorescence, Aestivation & Placentation (42 questions)
    fia = [
        (168, "Why does a racemose inflorescence exhibit indeterminate, continuous apical growth while a cymose inflorescence exhibits determinate growth?",
         {"A": "In racemose, the apical meristem remains active and continues growing; in cymose, the apical meristem terminates in a flower bud", "B": "In racemose, all flowers open simultaneously; in cymose, only one flower opens", "C": "Racemose is pollinated by wind; cymose is pollinated by insects", "D": "Racemose occurs only in gymnosperms; cymose occurs in angiosperms"},
         "A", "Indeterminate vs Determinate Growth in Inflorescence.",
         "1. In racemose inflorescences, the main axis continues to grow indefinitely because the apex never ends in a flower.\n2. In cymose inflorescences, the apical shoot apex differentiates into a terminal flower, halting further longitudinal growth of that axis.",
         "Options B, C, and D are scientifically incorrect.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Racemose vs Cymose Meristem"),

        (169, "In which type of racemose inflorescence are sessile flowers borne on an elongated peduncle in acropetal succession, as in Achyranthes?",
         {"A": "Spike", "B": "Raceme", "C": "Corymb", "D": "Umbel"},
         "A", "Spike Inflorescence.",
         "1. A spike is an unbranched racemose inflorescence bearing sessile (stalkless) flowers in acropetal succession (e.g., Achyranthes, Adhatoda).\n2. A raceme has pedicellate flowers (mustard).",
         "Corymb has pedicels bringing flowers to one level. Umbel has pedicels radiating from one point.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Spike Inflorescence"),

        (170, "A spadix inflorescence, characterized by a thick, fleshy peduncle bearing unisexual sessile flowers enclosed by one or more large brightly colored bracts (spathes), is typical of:",
         {"A": "Colocasia and maize", "B": "Mustard and radish", "C": "Sunflower and marigold", "D": "Coriander and carrot"},
         "A", "Spadix Inflorescence and Spathe.",
         "1. A spadix has a fleshy central axis covered with sessile unisexual flowers, surrounded by a large modified boat-shaped bract called a spathe (e.g., Colocasia, banana, maize female cob).",
         "Mustard has a raceme. Sunflower has a capitulum. Coriander has a compound umbel.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Spadix"),

        (171, "In the capitulum (head) inflorescence of sunflower (Asteraceae), what is the adaptive advantage of having two distinct types of florets?",
         {"A": "Peripheral sterile/pistillate ray florets with large ligulate corollas attract pollinators, while central bisexual disc florets produce seeds", "B": "Ray florets produce wind-borne pollen while disc florets store water", "C": "Ray florets develop into underground roots while disc florets make leaves", "D": "Ray florets prevent self-pollination by producing repellent toxins"},
         "A", "Division of Labour in Capitulum Florets.",
         "1. In the capitulum, the flat receptacle bears marginal zygomorphic ray florets that act as visual attractants for insects.\n2. The central actinomorphic disc florets are fertile and bisexual, performing pollination and seed production with maximum efficiency.",
         "Options B, C, and D are false.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Capitulum Division of Labour"),

        (172, "In a compound umbel inflorescence as seen in coriander (Coriandrum sativum) and carrot, all pedicels arise from:",
         {"A": "A single common point at the top of the peduncle, forming an umbrella-like cluster", "B": "Different nodes along a winding vine", "C": "Inside a hollow flask-shaped receptacle", "D": "The axils of underground rhizomatous scale leaves"},
         "A", "Umbel Inflorescence in Apiaceae.",
         "1. In an umbel, the main axis is condensed and pedicellate flowers radiate from a single common point like the ribs of an umbrella (e.g., coriander, fennel, carrot).",
         "Option C describes a hypanthodium (Ficus).",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Umbel Inflorescence"),

        (173, "The specialized cup-shaped inflorescence seen in Euphorbia, consisting of a single central female flower reduced to a pistil surrounded by multiple male flowers each reduced to a single stamen, is called a:",
         {"A": "Cyathium", "B": "Hypanthodium", "C": "Verticillaster", "D": "Capitulum"},
         "A", "Cyathium Inflorescence in Euphorbia.",
         "1. A cyathium looks like a single flower but is an inflorescence.\n2. Involucre bracts fuse into a cup with nectar glands; inside is one central achlamydeous female flower surrounded by scorpioid cymes of male flowers reduced to single pedicellate stamens.",
         "Hypanthodium is seen in Ficus. Verticillaster is seen in Ocimum.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Cyathium"),

        (174, "In Ficus (fig, banyan, peepal), the inflorescence is a fleshy, hollow, pear-shaped receptacle with an apical ostiole lined with downward-pointing hairs, called a:",
         {"A": "Hypanthodium", "B": "Cyathium", "C": "Verticillaster", "D": "Coenanthium"},
         "A", "Hypanthodium Inflorescence in Ficus.",
         "1. Hypanthodium is a specialized inflorescence where the fleshy receptacle forms a hollow flask-like cavity with an apical pore (ostiole).\n2. Inside are three types of flowers: male flowers near ostiole, female flowers at base, and sterile gall flowers pollinated by blastophaga wasps.",
         "Cyathium is in Euphorbia. Verticillaster is in Lamiaceae.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Hypanthodium"),

        (175, "The condensed false whorl of sessile flowers arising in the axils of opposite leaves in Ocimum (tulsi) and Salvia is a:",
         {"A": "Verticillaster", "B": "Cyathium", "C": "Hypanthodium", "D": "Spikelet"},
         "A", "Verticillaster Inflorescence in Lamiaceae.",
         "1. Verticillaster is a complex inflorescence where a pair of dichasial cymes arising in the axil of opposite leaves ends in monochasial cymes, forming a crowded false whorl around the stem node.",
         "Characteristics of family Lamiaceae (tulsi, mint, Salvia).",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Verticillaster"),

        (176, "How does a perigynous flower differ anatomically from an epigynous flower?",
         {"A": "In perigynous, the receptacle forms a cup/hypanthium that does NOT fuse with the ovary wall; in epigynous, the receptacle fuses completely with the ovary wall", "B": "In perigynous, the ovary is fully superior; in epigynous, it is half-superior", "C": "In perigynous, petals are absent; in epigynous, sepals are absent", "D": "In perigynous, the flower has bilateral symmetry; in epigynous, radial symmetry"},
         "A", "Anatomical Difference: Perigynous vs Epigynous.",
         "1. In perigynous flowers (plum, rose), the hypanthium cups around the ovary but remains free from its wall, keeping the ovary half-inferior.\n2. In epigynous flowers (guava, cucumber), the hypanthium wall is completely fused with the ovary wall, placing floral parts above it (inferior ovary).",
         "Options B, C, and D are inaccurate.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Perigynous vs Epigynous Anatomy"),

        (177, "Which of the following plants possess perigynous flowers with a half-inferior ovary?",
         {"A": "Peach, plum, and rose", "B": "Cucumber, guava, and sunflower", "C": "Mustard, radish, and brinjal", "D": "China rose, cotton, and tomato"},
         "A", "Perigynous Flower Species.",
         "1. Plum, peach, and rose (family Rosaceae) possess perigynous flowers with half-inferior ovaries.",
         "Option B is epigynous (inferior). Option C and D are hypogynous (superior).",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Perigynous Species"),

        (178, "In guava (Psidium guajava) and cucumber (Cucumis sativus), the floral parts are located on top of the ovary. This condition is:",
         {"A": "Epigynous with an inferior ovary", "B": "Hypogynous with a superior ovary", "C": "Perigynous with a half-inferior ovary", "D": "Gynandrous with an apocarpous ovary"},
         "A", "Epigynous Flowers in Guava and Cucumber.",
         "1. In guava and cucumber, the flowers are epigynous and the ovary is completely inferior.",
         "Superior ovary corresponds to hypogynous flowers.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Epigynous Condition"),

        (179, "Why is the calyx described as 'gamosepalous' in Solanaceae but 'polysepalous' in Brassicaceae?",
         {"A": "In Solanaceae sepals are united into a tube; in Brassicaceae sepals are completely free from one another", "B": "In Solanaceae sepals are photosynthetic; in Brassicaceae they are non-green petals", "C": "In Solanaceae sepals drop off before opening; in Brassicaceae they form the fruit wall", "D": "In Solanaceae sepals lack vascular supply; in Brassicaceae they possess cambium"},
         "A", "Gamosepalous vs Polysepalous.",
         "1. Gamosepalous means the sepals are united/fused along their margins (as in K(5) in Solanaceae).\n2. Polysepalous means the sepals remain distinct and free from each other (as in K2+2 in Brassicaceae).",
         "Options B, C, and D are false.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Gamosepalous vs Polysepalous"),

        (180, "What is the key distinguishing feature between twisted and imbricate aestivation?",
         {"A": "In twisted, overlapping is regular and unidirectional; in imbricate, overlapping is irregular without a specific direction", "B": "In twisted, margins do not touch; in imbricate, margins touch edge-to-edge", "C": "In twisted, there is a large posterior standard; in imbricate, all petals are identical", "D": "Twisted occurs only in monocots; imbricate occurs only in gymnosperms"},
         "A", "Twisted vs Imbricate Aestivation.",
         "1. In twisted aestivation, every petal has one margin internal and one margin external, overlapping in a consistent circular order.\n2. In imbricate aestivation, typically one petal is completely internal, one is completely external, and the remaining petals have one margin internal and one external.",
         "Option B describes valvate. Option C describes vexillary.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Twisted vs Imbricate"),

        (181, "In Cassia and Delonix regia (gulmohur), which type of aestivation is observed in the corolla?",
         {"A": "Imbricate", "B": "Twisted", "C": "Valvate", "D": "Vexillary"},
         "A", "Imbricate in Cassia and Gulmohur.",
         "1. Both Cassia and Delonix regia (gulmohur) display imbricate aestivation where petal margins overlap irregularly without a single directional rotation.",
         "Calotropis is valvate. China rose is twisted. Pea is vexillary.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Cassia Aestivation"),

        (182, "In vexillary aestivation of family Fabaceae, how are the five petals arranged and designated from posterior to anterior?",
         {"A": "1 posterior standard (vexillum) + 2 lateral wings (alae) + 2 fused anterior keel (carina)", "B": "2 posterior wings + 2 lateral standards + 1 anterior keel", "C": "5 identical petals arranged in a simple twisted whorl", "D": "3 outer sepals fused with 2 inner staminodes"},
         "A", "Vexillary Corolla Architecture.",
         "1. Formula: C1+2+(2).\n2. Outermost posterior petal is the standard (vexillum); lateral two are wings (alae); innermost two anterior petals are united to form the boat-shaped keel (carina) enclosing stamens and pistil.",
         "Options B, C, and D are wrong representations.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Vexillary Architecture"),

        (183, "In a pea flower, which petals enclose and protect the essential reproductive organs (stamens and carpel)?",
         {"A": "Keel (carina)", "B": "Standard (vexillum)", "C": "Wings (alae)", "D": "Epicalyx"},
         "A", "Keel Enclosing Reproductive Organs.",
         "1. The two anterior fused petals forming the keel or carina completely envelop the ten stamens and the monocarpellary pistil, promoting self-pollination.",
         "Standard attracts pollinators. Wings provide a landing platform for bees.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Keel Protection"),

        (184, "The condition where stamens are fused with each other by both their anthers and their filaments throughout their entire length, as in Cucurbita, is called:",
         {"A": "Synandrous", "B": "Syngenesious", "C": "Monadelphous", "D": "Diadelphous"},
         "A", "Synandrous Stamens.",
         "1. In the synandrous condition, stamens are fused completely along their entire length (both filaments and anthers are united), as seen in male flowers of Cucurbita.",
         "Syngenesious means only anthers are fused while filaments remain free (Asteraceae). Monadelphous means only filaments are fused.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Synandrous"),

        (185, "In family Asteraceae (sunflower), stamens have their anthers united into a tube while their filaments remain completely free. This condition is termed:",
         {"A": "Syngenesious", "B": "Synandrous", "C": "Polyadelphous", "D": "Diadelphous"},
         "A", "Syngenesious Stamens in Asteraceae.",
         "1. In Asteraceae disc florets, the 5 anthers fuse together into a hollow cylinder surrounding the style, but filaments are free and epipetalous.\n2. This is termed syngenesious and facilitates the plunger pollination mechanism.",
         "Synandrous fuses both. Polyadelphous fuses filaments into bunches.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Syngenesious"),

        (186, "In Calotropis, stamens are fused with the stigmatic disc of the gynoecium to form a specialized composite reproductive structure termed the:",
         {"A": "Gynostegium", "B": "Gynophore", "C": "Androphore", "D": "Pistillode"},
         "A", "Gynostegium in Calotropis (Asclepiadaceae/Apocynaceae).",
         "1. In Calotropis, stamens fuse with the pentangular stigmatic disc to form a complex apparatus called the gynostegium.\n2. Pollen grains of each anther lobe adhere into coherent masses called pollinia.",
         "Gynophore is an internodal stalk elevating the ovary above the receptacle.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Gynostegium"),

        (187, "What is a pollinium as found in Calotropis and orchids?",
         {"A": "A coherent, waxy mass of all pollen grains produced within an anther locule", "B": "A specialized glandular bract secreting sugary nectar", "C": "A sterile carpel that mimics a female insect", "D": "A modified seed equipped with a silky coma of hairs"},
         "A", "Definition of Pollinium.",
         "1. In orchids and Calotropis, individual pollen grains do not separate at maturity.\n2. Instead, all pollen grains of an anther lobe stick together to form a discrete structure called a pollinium, transported as a single unit by insect vectors.",
         "Options B, C, and D are unrelated structures.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Pollinium"),

        (188, "In Ocimum and Salvia, the androecium consists of 4 stamens, of which 2 are long and 2 are short. This stamen arrangement is termed:",
         {"A": "Didynamous", "B": "Tetradynamous", "C": "Diadelphous", "D": "Polyadelphous"},
         "A", "Didynamous Condition in Lamiaceae.",
         "1. Didynamous: 4 stamens in two pairs of unequal lengths (2 long + 2 short), characteristic of family Lamiaceae (Ocimum, Salvia).\n2. Tetradynamous: 6 stamens (4 long + 2 short), characteristic of Brassicaceae.",
         "Option B is 6 stamens. Option C is 2 bundles.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Didynamous"),

        (189, "In mustard (Brassica), the tetradynamous androecium consists of:",
         {"A": "Six stamens: two outer short stamens and four inner long stamens", "B": "Four stamens: two outer long and two inner short", "C": "Six stamens: three outer short and three inner long", "D": "Eight stamens arranged in two equal whorls"},
         "A", "Tetradynamous Stamens in Brassica.",
         "1. In mustard and other crucifers, there are 6 stamens: the outer whorl contains 2 shorter stamens, while the inner whorl contains 4 longer stamens (2+4 arrangement).",
         "Didynamous has 4 stamens (2+2).",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Tetradynamous Detail"),

        (190, "In a flower, an internodal elongation between the corolla and the androecium that elevates the stamens is called an:",
         {"A": "Androphore", "B": "Gynophore", "C": "Androgynophore", "D": "Anthophore"},
         "A", "Androphore.",
         "1. Anthophore: internode between calyx and corolla (Silene).\n2. Androphore: internode between corolla and androecium (Passiflora).\n3. Gynophore: internode between androecium and gynoecium (Capparis).\n4. Androgynophore: internode elevating both androecium and gynoecium (Grewia).",
         "Gynophore elevates the ovary. Anthophore elevates corolla.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Androphore"),

        (191, "An internodal elongation between the androecium and the gynoecium that elevates the ovary above the stamens, as in Capparis, is a:",
         {"A": "Gynophore", "B": "Androphore", "C": "Pedicel", "D": "Thalamus"},
         "A", "Gynophore in Capparis.",
         "1. A gynophore is a specialized elongated stalk or internode on the thalamus that elevates the gynoecium/pistil above the stamens (e.g., Capparis, Cleome).",
         "Option B elevates stamens. Option C is flower stalk.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Gynophore"),

        (192, "Why is the ovary in mustard and Argemone described as unilocular initially but becoming bilocular later?",
         {"A": "Because a false longitudinal septum called the replum develops between the parietal placentae", "B": "Because two carpels fuse only after fertilization occurs", "C": "Because the ovary wall invaginates at its base to divide the locule into two", "D": "Because the central placenta expands radially outward to touch the pericarp"},
         "A", "Replum Formation in Parietal Placentation.",
         "1. Mustard and Argemone have bicarpellary syncarpous ovaries with parietal placentation.\n2. Although the ovary is structurally one-chambered (unilocular), a non-carpellary false partition called the replum grows inward between the parietal placentae, making the mature fruit two-chambered (bilocular).",
         "Options B, C, and D are embryologically incorrect.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Replum Mechanism"),

        (193, "How does free central placentation differ from axile placentation?",
         {"A": "In free central, the ovary is unilocular with ovules borne on a central column and septa are absent; in axile, the ovary is divided into multiple locules by true septa", "B": "In free central, ovules are attached to the ovary wall; in axile, ovules float freely in mucilage", "C": "In free central, the ovary is always inferior; in axile, it is always superior", "D": "In free central, only a single ovule is present at the base; in axile, millions of ovules are present"},
         "A", "Free Central vs Axile Placentation.",
         "1. In axile placentation (tomato, lemon), radial septa divide the ovary into 2, 3, or more locules, and ovules attach where septa meet at the centre.\n2. In free central placentation (Dianthus, Primrose), radial septa are completely absent, making the ovary unilocular, and ovules attach directly to a central vertical column.",
         "Option D describes basal placentation.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Free Central vs Axile"),

        (194, "In basal placentation, characteristic of sunflower and marigold, what is the number of locules and ovules per ovary?",
         {"A": "Unilocular ovary containing a single ovule attached at the base", "B": "Bilocular ovary containing four ovules attached at the centre", "C": "Multilocular ovary containing numerous ovules in two rows", "D": "Tri-locular ovary containing three ovules attached to parietal walls"},
         "A", "Characteristics of Basal Placentation.",
         "1. Basal placentation occurs in a unilocular, bicarpellary syncarpous inferior ovary containing exactly one ovule attached to the base of the ovary cavity (e.g., Asteraceae: sunflower, marigold).",
         "Options B, C, and D describe multilocular or multi-ovulate conditions.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Basal Placentation Details"),

        (195, "Which type of placentation is found in an ovary that is monocarpellary, unilocular, and bears ovules along the ventral suture, as seen in legumes?",
         {"A": "Marginal placentation", "B": "Axile placentation", "C": "Parietal placentation", "D": "Free central placentation"},
         "A", "Marginal Placentation Architecture.",
         "1. Marginal placentation occurs in a simple monocarpellary pistil where the placenta develops along the margin where carpel edges meet (ventral suture).\n2. Ovules are arranged in two alternate rows (e.g., pea, bean, gram).",
         "Axile and parietal occur in multicarpellary ovaries.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Marginal Architecture"),

        (196, "Match the type of placentation with its representative plant:\n(1) Marginal — (p) Dianthus\n(2) Axile — (q) Pea\n(3) Parietal — (r) Lemon\n(4) Free central — (s) Argemone",
         {"A": "1-q, 2-r, 3-s, 4-p", "B": "1-q, 2-p, 3-s, 4-r", "C": "1-r, 2-q, 3-p, 4-s", "D": "1-p, 2-s, 3-r, 4-q"},
         "A", "Placentation Matching.",
         "1. Marginal: Pea (1-q).\n2. Axile: Lemon, tomato, China rose (2-r).\n3. Parietal: Argemone, mustard (3-s).\n4. Free central: Dianthus, Primrose (4-p).",
         "Thus, 1-q, 2-r, 3-s, 4-p is the correct combination.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Placentation Matching"),

        (197, "The epicalyx, a whorl of bracteole-like appendages situated immediately below the calyx, is a distinctive diagnostic feature of family:",
         {"A": "Malvaceae (e.g., China rose, lady's finger)", "B": "Fabaceae", "C": "Solanaceae", "D": "Liliaceae"},
         "A", "Epicalyx in Malvaceae.",
         "1. Malvaceae members characteristically possess an epicalyx (an extra outer calyx formed by a whorl of green sepal-like bracteoles) below the true calyx (e.g., Hibiscus, Abelmoschus, Gossypium).",
         "Other families lack an epicalyx.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Epicalyx in Malvaceae"),

        (198, "In Malvaceae, anthers are unilocular (monothecous) and reniform (kidney-shaped). How does this differ from typical angiosperm anthers?",
         {"A": "Typical angiosperm anthers are dithecous (bilocular) with four microsporangia, whereas Malvaceae anthers are monothecous with two microsporangia", "B": "Typical anthers lack pollen grains; Malvaceae anthers have millions", "C": "Typical anthers are sterile staminodes; Malvaceae anthers are carpelloid", "D": "Typical anthers produce spores via mitosis only"},
         "A", "Monothecous Anthers in Malvaceae.",
         "1. A typical angiosperm anther is dithecous (two-lobed) and tetrasporangiate.\n2. In Malvaceae, each stamen possesses only one anther lobe (monothecous, bisporangiate) which is characteristically reniform (kidney-shaped).",
         "Options B, C, and D are false.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Monothecous Anther"),

        (199, "What type of symmetry is shown by the flowers of Canna?",
         {"A": "Asymmetric (irregular)", "B": "Actinomorphic (radial)", "C": "Zygomorphic (bilateral)", "D": "Biradial"},
         "A", "Asymmetric Flower in Canna.",
         "1. Canna flowers cannot be divided into two equal halves by any vertical plane passing through the centre, exhibiting asymmetric symmetry.",
         "Mustard and chilli are actinomorphic. Pea and bean are zygomorphic.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Canna Symmetry"),

        (200, "In which of the following plants are flowers trimerous, with floral parts in multiples of three?",
         {"A": "Liliaceae (monocots like lily, onion, tulip)", "B": "Fabaceae (dicots like pea, gram)", "C": "Solanaceae (dicots like potato, tomato)", "D": "Brassicaceae (dicots like mustard)"},
         "A", "Trimerous Flowers in Liliaceae.",
         "1. Monocotyledons typically possess trimerous flowers where floral appendages occur in multiples of three (e.g., P3+3, A3+3, G(3) in Liliaceae).\n2. Dicots typically possess tetramerous (Brassicaceae) or pentamerous (Fabaceae, Solanaceae) flowers.",
         "Options B, C, and D are pentamerous or tetramerous dicots.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Trimerous Flowers"),

        (201, "A flower that possesses both androecium and gynoecium is termed bisexual (hermaphrodite), whereas a flower having either only stamens or only carpels is:",
         {"A": "Unisexual", "B": "Neuter", "C": "Achlamydeous", "D": "Bracteate"},
         "A", "Unisexual Flowers.",
         "1. A flower bearing only stamens is staminate (male unisexual).\n2. A flower bearing only carpels is pistillate (female unisexual).\n3. When both are absent, it is neuter.",
         "Achlamydeous lacks calyx and corolla. Bracteate has bracts.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Unisexual"),

        (202, "If a flower has reduced green leaves found at the base of the pedicel, it is designated as:",
         {"A": "Bracteate", "B": "Ebracteate", "C": "Bracteolate", "D": "Petaloid"},
         "A", "Bracteate Flowers.",
         "1. Bracts are reduced leaf-like structures found at the base of the pedicel. Flowers with bracts are called bracteate (Bracteate/Br).\n2. Flowers lacking them are ebracteate (Ebr).",
         "Option B means lacking bracts. Option C refers to appendages on pedicel itself.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Bracteate"),

        (203, "In which family are the flowers typically pentamerous, actinomorphic, with persistent calyx and bicarpellary obliquely placed ovary?",
         {"A": "Solanaceae", "B": "Fabaceae", "C": "Liliaceae", "D": "Poaceae"},
         "A", "Diagnostic Characters of Solanaceae.",
         "1. Solanaceae flowers are pentamerous, actinomorphic, with a persistent calyx, epipetalous stamens, and a bicarpellary syncarpous superior ovary placed obliquely in the flower.",
         "Fabaceae is zygomorphic. Liliaceae is trimerous.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Solanaceae Diagnostic"),

        (204, "Why is the ovary described as 'obliquely placed' in family Solanaceae?",
         {"A": "The posterior carpel is tilted towards the right and the anterior carpel towards the left at an angle of roughly 45 degrees", "B": "The ovary hangs upside down from the pedicel", "C": "The ovary is embedded inside the floral cup diagonally", "D": "The ovary is rotated 180 degrees by resupination as in orchids"},
         "A", "Oblique Ovary in Solanaceae.",
         "1. In Solanaceae, the vertical plane of the two carpels does not coincide with the median plane of the flower.\n2. Instead, the carpels lie at an oblique angle of approximately 45 degrees (posterior carpel tilted to the right, anterior to the left), accompanied by a swollen placenta.",
         "Options B, C, and D are incorrect.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Oblique Ovary"),

        (205, "The swollen placenta bearing numerous ovules is a hallmark feature of which family?",
         {"A": "Solanaceae", "B": "Fabaceae", "C": "Brassicaceae", "D": "Asteraceae"},
         "A", "Swollen Placenta in Solanaceae.",
         "1. In Solanaceae, the placenta is distinctly swollen and bears numerous ovules on axile placentation within the two locules.",
         "Fabaceae has marginal placentation. Brassicaceae has parietal placentation. Asteraceae has basal placentation.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Swollen Placenta"),

        (206, "In Fabaceae, what is the number of carpels and locules in the ovary?",
         {"A": "Monocarpellary and unilocular with superior ovary", "B": "Bicarpellary and bilocular with inferior ovary", "C": "Tricarpellary and trilocular with superior ovary", "D": "Polycarpellary and apocarpous with inferior ovary"},
         "A", "Gynoecium of Fabaceae.",
         "1. Gynoecium of Fabaceae is monocarpellary (single carpel), unilocular (single chamber), with a superior ovary and marginal placentation bearing many ovules.",
         "Option B is Solanaceae. Option C is Liliaceae.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Fabaceae Gynoecium"),

        (207, "In Liliaceae, the gynoecium is characterized as:",
         {"A": "Tricarpellary, syncarpous, superior ovary, trilocular with axile placentation", "B": "Monocarpellary, unilocular, superior ovary with marginal placentation", "C": "Bicarpellary, syncarpous, inferior ovary with parietal placentation", "D": "Polycarpellary, apocarpous, superior ovary with basal placentation"},
         "A", "Gynoecium of Liliaceae.",
         "1. Liliaceae gynoecium is tricarpellary, syncarpous, superior ovary, trilocular with many ovules on axile placentation (G(3)).",
         "Option B is Fabaceae. Option C is Brassicaceae/Asteraceae.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Liliaceae Gynoecium"),

        (208, "Which type of style arises from the base of the ovary between the four lobes of the gynoecium in family Lamiaceae (tulsi)?",
         {"A": "Gynobasic style", "B": "Terminal style", "C": "Lateral style", "D": "Sub-apical style"},
         "A", "Gynobasic Style in Lamiaceae.",
         "1. A gynobasic style arises from a depression in the centre of the ovary lobes, originating directly from the base of the ovary rather than from the apex of carpels (characteristic of Lamiaceae/Boraginaceae).",
         "Terminal style arises from apex of ovary (standard in most angiosperms).",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Gynobasic Style"),

        (209, "In Asteraceae (sunflower), the calyx is modified into a ring of persistent fine hairs or bristles that aids in wind dispersal of the fruit. This structure is the:",
         {"A": "Pappus", "B": "Epicalyx", "C": "Perianth", "D": "Involucre"},
         "A", "Pappus Calyx in Asteraceae.",
         "1. In Asteraceae, the sepals are reduced or modified into a ring of hair-like bristles or scales called the pappus.\n2. The pappus remains attached to the single-seeded cypsela fruit and acts as a parachute for anemochorous wind dispersal.",
         "Epicalyx is in Malvaceae. Involucre is the whorl of bracts.",
         "Medium", "bio-flower-inflorescence-placentation", "Inflorescence, Flower, Aestivation & Placentation", "Pappus"),
    ]
